faq:
  - q: How Much Does Routine Maintenance Cost vs. a Major Repair?
    body: >-
      Every routine maintenance estimate at Duft Auto comes in writing before service begins, so the cost of a fluid, filter, or belt service is clear before any decision gets made. A scheduled fluid change or filter swap costs a fraction of the repair that follows when that same service gets deferred past its interval — a neglected belt or degraded transmission fluid can escalate into an engine or transmission repair instead of a routine line item. Duft Auto walks through which items are actually due at the current mileage before ordering any parts, rather than selling a standard package regardless of vehicle condition. Vehicles serviced on schedule rarely reach the point where a major component requires full replacement.
  - q: How Do I Know When My Vehicle Needs Routine Maintenance?
    items:
      - Dashboard maintenance reminder light illuminated
      - Mileage approaching the manufacturer-published interval
      - Squeaking or cracking sound from a belt
      - Fluid appears low, discolored, or contaminated
      - Rough idle, delayed shifting, or reduced fuel efficiency
  - q: What Does the Routine Maintenance Process Involve?
    body: >-
      A routine maintenance visit starts with a scheduled appointment — no walk-in wait, no guessing when a bay opens up. Auggie checks the vehicle's mileage against its manufacturer-published interval to confirm which fluids, filters, and belts are due. A multi-point inspection covers brakes, tires, suspension, and battery condition alongside the scheduled service items, with findings recorded in writing. Any component approaching the end of its service life gets flagged and explained in plain language before work proceeds. Same-day turnaround applies to most fluid and filter service, while belt replacement or a larger inspection finding gets a realistic timeline up front.
